<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en-GB">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Get your business ready to sell | Arrington Consultancy</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Practical commercial work to make an established business stronger, less dependent on its owner and straightforward for somebody else to take on. Starts with a conversation or a £500 Commercial Review.">
    <link rel="canonical" href="https://www.arringtonconsultancy.com/get-your-business-ready-to-sell">
    <meta property="og:type" content="website">
    <meta property="og:title" content="Get your business ready to sell | Arrington Consultancy">
    <meta property="og:description" content="A business that can run without you is easier for somebody else to take on, and better to own while you still have it.">
    <meta property="og:url" content="https://www.arringtonconsultancy.com/get-your-business-ready-to-sell">
    <meta property="og:image" content="https://www.arringtonconsultancy.com/img/logo">
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image">
    <meta name="twitter:title" content="Get your business ready to sell | Arrington Consultancy">
    <meta name="twitter:description" content="A business that can run without you is easier for somebody else to take on, and better to own while you still have it.">
    <meta name="twitter:image" content="https://www.arringtonconsultancy.com/img/logo">
    <meta name="csrf-token" content="<%= csrfToken %>">
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico" sizes="any">
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com">
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin>
    <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=DM+Sans:ital,wght@0,400;0,500;0,700;1,400&family=DM+Serif+Display&display=swap" rel="stylesheet">
    <%# The site-wide Ads/GA4 base tag every other page carries. No new
        conversion action and no new label is introduced here: the only
        conversions this page can fire are the two existing site-wide ones in
        site-chrome-script (contact click, contact form submit), exactly as on
        every other page. Tom's build instruction of 25/09/2026 is that Google
        Ads campaigns and settings are not changed by this build, and they are
        not. %>
    <script async src="https://www.googletagmanager.com/gtag/js?id=AW-18129914078"></script>
    <script nonce="<%= nonce %>">
        window.dataLayer = window.dataLayer || [];
        function gtag(){dataLayer.push(arguments);}
        gtag('js', new Date());
        gtag('config', 'AW-18129914078');
        <% if (ga4Id) { %>
        gtag('config', '<%= ga4Id %>');
        <% } %>
    </script>
    <%# Deliberately NO Product/Offer schema partial here, unlike the four
        Where to Start offer pages. Those describe a purchasable offer at a
        published price. This page introduces no new priced offer, so marking
        one up would assert a product that does not exist. The £500 Commercial
        Review keeps its own schema on its own page. %>
    <style nonce="<%= nonce %>">
        :root {
            <% for (const [prop, val] of Object.entries(theme.vars)) { %>
            <%= prop %>: <%= val %>;
            <% } %>
        }
        * { box-sizing: border-box; }
        body { margin: 0; font-family: 'DM Sans', sans-serif; background: var(--black); color: var(--text); line-height: 1.6; }
        <%- include('partials/site-chrome-styles') %>

        h1, h2, h3 { font-family: 'DM Serif Display', serif; font-weight: 400; color: var(--text-bright); line-height: 1.2; margin: 0; }
        p { color: var(--text); margin: 0 0 1.2rem; }

        .btn { display: inline-block; font-family: 'DM Sans', sans-serif; font-size: 0.92rem; font-weight: 600; padding: 0.6rem 1.5rem; border-radius: 3px; text-decoration: none; cursor: pointer; border: 1px solid transparent; }
        .btn-primary { background: var(--accent); color: var(--black); border-color: var(--accent); }
        .btn-primary:hover, .btn-primary:focus-visible { background: var(--accent-hover); border-color: var(--accent-hover); }
        /* Same reasoning as the Commercial Review page: the generic outline
           border is dim enough on the near-black ground to read as a disabled
           box, so the secondary route carries the accent at rest. The fill is
           what separates it from the primary CTA, which is the site's existing
           button language rather than a new one. */
        .btn-outline { background: transparent; color: var(--accent); border-color: var(--accent); }
        .btn-outline:hover, .btn-outline:focus-visible { background: var(--accent); color: var(--black); }
        /* No min-height or flex centring is declared for the buttons here on
           purpose. site-chrome-styles already sets both site-wide via
           `a.btn, button.btn`, element-qualified so it deliberately outranks
           any page's own bare `.btn`, and measuring the rendered page confirms
           it reaches these buttons (44px, flex-centred). A 48px rule here
           would be dead code: it loses that cascade, so the comment beside it
           would describe a height the page does not have. Both routes are
           therefore the same height already, and the difference in weight is
           the fill rather than the size. */

        main { max-width: 700px; margin: 0 auto; padding: calc(var(--site-chrome) + 2.5rem) 1.5rem 5rem; }

        .sr-label { font-size: 0.72rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.14em; font-weight: 700; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 1rem; display: block; }
        h1 { font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 4.5vw, 2.4rem); margin-bottom: 1.2rem; }
        .sr-lede { font-size: 1.12rem; color: var(--text-bright); margin-bottom: 0; }

        /* OPERATOR CREDIBILITY, HIGH ON THE PAGE.
           De-boxed on purpose: hairline rules above and below rather than a
           filled card, which is the "editorial, fewer-containers" treatment
           the fourcards/credentials/proof strip sections already use on the
           main site. A box here would compete with the two route cards
           directly underneath, which are the thing the visitor has to choose
           between. Every sentence in it is controlled copy already live
           elsewhere on the site; see the EJS comment on the block itself. */
        .sr-cred {
            margin-top: 2.5rem;
            padding: 1.5rem 0;
            border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
            border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
        }
        .sr-cred p { margin: 0; font-size: 1.02rem; color: var(--text-bright); }
        .sr-cred p + p { margin-top: 0.5rem; color: var(--text); font-size: 0.97rem; }

        .sr-section { margin-top: 3.25rem; }
        .sr-section > h2 { font-size: 1.4rem; margin-bottom: 0.9rem; }
        .sr-section > .sr-intro { font-size: 1rem; color: var(--text-muted); margin-bottom: 1.6rem; max-width: 58ch; }

        /* THE TWO ROUTES, side by side so the choice is visible without
           reading. Each card leads with an accent condition label, so a
           visitor matches their own situation on four or five words rather
           than on a paragraph. Same bordered-card device as Owner Check's
           .check-card and the Where to Start offer pages. */
        .sr-routes { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); gap: 1.25rem; }
        .sr-route {
            border: 1px solid var(--border);
            border-radius: 6px;
            padding: 1.75rem 1.5rem;
            display: flex;
            flex-direction: column;
        }
        /* EQUAL WEIGHT AT REST, deliberately. These two cards are not a
           primary and a secondary choice: they are two equally valid routes,
           and which one applies is decided by the visitor's situation, not by
           us. So neither card carries an accent edge and both buttons use the
           same treatment.

           It used to work the other way. The already-selling card had
           border-color: color-mix(accent 45%, border) and a solid btn-primary,
           on the reasoning that the conversation is the primary CTA site-wide.
           Tom inspected the live page on 25/09/2026 and called it: emphasis on
           the ROUTE is indistinguishable, on screen, from a claim that one
           reader's situation matters more. Moving between the two cards showed
           two different highlighted states, so whichever card the pointer was
           over looked like the chosen one. */
        /* 0.72rem matches this page's own .sr-label and the site's existing
           small-label convention (Owner Check's .check-card-learn is 0.75rem).
           It was 0.68rem, which measured 10.88px on the rendered page and was
           the smallest text anywhere on the site. */
        .sr-route-label {
            font-size: 0.72rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.12em;
            font-weight: 700; color: var(--accent); margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
        }
        .sr-route h3 { font-size: 1.2rem; margin-bottom: 0.6rem; }
        .sr-route p { font-size: 0.97rem; margin-bottom: 1.4rem; }
        .sr-route-actions { margin-top: auto; }
        /* WHOLE-CARD CLICKABLE. The anchor stays the only link; its ::after is
           stretched over the card, so a click anywhere in the card activates
           that same anchor. See the EJS comment on the markup for why this is
           done here rather than by wrapping the card or adding a JS handler. */
        <%# background-color was in this transition list until 25/09/2026 and
            is dropped with the hover fill it existed for: the card's
            background no longer changes in any state, so transitioning it
            animated nothing and implied a fill someone might go looking for. %>
        .sr-route { position: relative; cursor: pointer; transition: border-color 0.2s; }
        .sr-route-link::after {
            content: '';
            position: absolute;
            inset: 0;
            border-radius: 6px;
        }
        /* HOVER IS AN AFFORDANCE, NOT A SELECTION. The card still has to read
           as clickable before the visitor finds the button, so the border
           brightens and the cursor changes. That is all it does.

           Three things used to happen on hover and together they made the
           hovered card look chosen: the border went to full var(--accent), a
           5% accent wash filled the card, and the button was repainted (the
           outline button FILLED SOLID GOLD, the primary button darkened). The
           fill was the worst of them, because a solid gold button is exactly
           how this site marks a primary action, so hovering the left card
           promoted it and leaving promoted the right one instead.

           The border now goes to a mid-strength mix rather than the full
           accent, so it is visibly warmer without looking like the focus ring
           below it.

           THE STRETCHED LINK MAKES CARD HOVER AND BUTTON HOVER THE SAME
           EVENT, and deleting .sr-route:hover .btn-outline did NOT fix the
           fill. The anchor's ::after covers the whole card, so a pointer
           anywhere on the card is over the ANCHOR, and the site's own
           .btn-outline:hover fires from the far corner of the card. Reading
           the stylesheet said this was fixed; a computed-style check in a real
           browser showed the button still painting rgb(196,122,58) with black
           text. So the fill is cancelled explicitly for these two buttons,
           which is the whole point of the correction: no state reachable by
           pointing at a card may look like choosing it.

           :focus-visible deliberately KEEPS the fill, and is declared after
           so it wins when a button is both focused and hovered. Hover is
           decoration and could be dropped; a visible focus state is how a
           keyboard user knows where they are. */
        .sr-route:hover { border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 55%, var(--border)); }
        .sr-route-link:hover { background: transparent; color: var(--accent); }
        .sr-route-link:focus-visible { background: var(--accent); color: var(--black); }
        /* Keyboard focus lands on the button, which is inside the card, so the
           ring is drawn at CARD level. Without this a keyboard user would see
           a focus ring on a button while the whole card is what activates.
           Deliberately kept at FULL accent plus an outline: unlike hover, a
           focus ring must be unmistakable, and only one card can hold focus
           at a time so there is no hierarchy to imply. */
        .sr-route:focus-within { border-color: var(--accent); outline: 2px solid var(--accent); outline-offset: 3px; }
        @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { .sr-route { transition: none; } }
        /* Reduced side padding inside the route cards only. At the 700px
           column each card is about 337px wide, and the site's standard
           1.5rem button padding left too little for "Book a 30 minute
           conversation" to sit on one line, so both CTAs wrapped to two.
           Measured on the rendered page, not guessed. The button's height,
           colour and weight are untouched, so this is a fit correction
           rather than a second button style. */
        .sr-route-actions .btn { width: 100%; text-align: center; padding-left: 0.75rem; padding-right: 0.75rem; }

        /* WHAT A BUYER ASKS: five points since 25/09/2026, previously six.
           De-boxed grid, top rule per item, exactly the .fourcard treatment
           from index.ejs: the lead-in and the rule carry the separation
           instead of more boxes. Deliberately NOT numbered, because these are
           not a sequence and a number would assert an order that does not
           exist. */
        .sr-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); gap: 1.5rem 2rem; }
        /* An odd last item runs the full width of the grid rather than sitting
           alone in the left column looking left over. Tom's decision of
           25/09/2026: no sixth point manufactured to fill the slot. Written
           as a condition on the count, not as "the fifth item", so it stays
           right if the list ever changes length. On mobile the grid is a
           single column already, so this changes nothing there. */
        .sr-grid > .sr-item:last-child:nth-child(odd) { grid-column: 1 / -1; }
        /* Gold rules rather than grey (Tom, 25/09/2026), so the section carries
           the brand without the items turning into cards. 40% is the same
           accent strength the site already uses for a restrained accent
           hairline on .case-more a in index.ejs, so this is the existing
           treatment rather than a new one. The items stay unboxed: a rule
           only, no border, background or radius, because bordered boxes
           would read as calls to action rather than supporting detail. */
        .sr-item { padding-top: 1.1rem; border-top: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 40%, transparent); }
        .sr-item-lead { display: block; font-weight: 700; color: var(--text-bright); margin-bottom: 0.3rem; font-size: 0.98rem; }
        .sr-item p { font-size: 0.94rem; color: var(--text-muted); margin: 0; }

        /* Accent-dot list, lifted from Owner Check's .check-card ul rather
           than using the browser's default discs, which are the one thing
           that made the first version read as an unstyled document. */
        .sr-dots { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; }
        .sr-dots li { position: relative; padding-left: 1.15rem; margin-bottom: 0.85rem; font-size: 0.98rem; color: var(--text); }
        .sr-dots li:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
        .sr-dots li::before {
            content: ''; position: absolute; left: 0; top: 0.6em;
            width: 5px; height: 5px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--accent);
        }
        .sr-dots strong { color: var(--text-bright); font-weight: 700; }
        .sr-dots--two { columns: 2; column-gap: 2rem; }
        .sr-dots--two li { break-inside: avoid; }

        /* The review: two numbered steps, because this genuinely IS a
           sequence. The price is pulled out of the sequence rather than
           becoming a third step, since paying is not a stage of the work. */
        /* The one image on the page. Radius matches the cards so it belongs to
           the same visual system, and width/height on the element plus
           height:auto here keeps the reserved space correct at every width
           without a layout jump when it loads. */
        .sr-figure {
            display: block;
            width: 100%;
            height: auto;
            border-radius: 6px;
            margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
        }

        .sr-card { border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 6px; padding: 1.9rem 2rem; }
        .sr-step { display: grid; grid-template-columns: auto 1fr; gap: 1rem; }
        .sr-step + .sr-step { margin-top: 1.5rem; padding-top: 1.5rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--border); }
        .sr-step-num { font-family: 'DM Serif Display', serif; font-size: 1.6rem; color: var(--accent); opacity: 0.45; line-height: 1; }
        .sr-step h3 { font-size: 1.1rem; margin-bottom: 0.4rem; }
        .sr-step p { font-size: 0.97rem; margin: 0; }
        .sr-price {
            margin-top: 1.25rem; padding-top: 1.25rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
            display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 0.9rem; flex-wrap: wrap;
        }
        .sr-price-fig { font-family: 'DM Serif Display', serif; font-size: 1.9rem; color: var(--accent); line-height: 1; }
        .sr-price p { margin: 0; font-size: 0.94rem; color: var(--text-muted); }

        /* WE STAY IN YOUR CORNER, directly on the page background. This was
           the page's one cream paper card until Tom removed it after a live
           inspection on 25/09/2026: too heavy against the navy, and it made
           the section read like a generic information card. So there is no
           container here, and there must not be one.

           What replaces the card, all of it restrained:
           - the ordinary body text (var(--text) on var(--black), 12.08:1 on
             the live Oxford theme, well above AAA);
           - a readable measure, so the six paragraphs do not run the full
             width of the column;
           - a short gold rule under the heading, the only accent, in the
             brand gold at the same 2px weight a button border reads at;
           - spacing for hierarchy: more room above the section than its
             neighbours get, because it follows another all-text section and
             the card used to supply that break, and more room above the
             closing paragraph, which is the section's concluding thought.
           Specificity is doubled (.sr-section.sr-corner) so these win over
           the generic .sr-section rules in either media query, whatever
           order they appear in. */
        .sr-section.sr-corner { margin-top: 4.25rem; }
        .sr-section.sr-corner > h2 { margin-bottom: 1.4rem; }
        .sr-section.sr-corner > h2::after {
            content: '';
            display: block;
            width: 2.5rem;
            height: 2px;
            margin-top: 0.9rem;
            background: var(--accent);
        }
        .sr-corner-body { max-width: 62ch; }
        .sr-corner-body p:last-child { margin-top: 1.9rem; margin-bottom: 0; }
        @media (max-width: 760px) {
            .sr-section.sr-corner { margin-top: 2.9rem; }
            .sr-section.sr-corner > h2 { margin-bottom: 1.15rem; }
            .sr-corner-body p:last-child { margin-top: 1.5rem; }
        }

        .sr-final { border-top: 1px solid var(--border); padding-top: 2.5rem; margin-top: 3.5rem; }
        .sr-final h2 { font-size: 1.4rem; margin-bottom: 0.6rem; }
        .sr-final-actions { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.9rem; margin-top: 1.6rem; }
        .sr-aside { font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--text-muted); margin-top: 1.6rem; }
        /* An inline link in body copy. Nothing in site-chrome-styles sets a
           colour for one, so without this the Evidence link rendered in
           default browser blue and underlined on the near-black ground, which
           reads as an unstyled page rather than an Arrington one. This is the
           site's existing treatment, copied from .case-more a in index.ejs
           rather than invented here. */
        .sr-aside a {
            color: var(--accent);
            text-decoration: none;
            border-bottom: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 40%, transparent);
            padding-bottom: 2px;
        }
        .sr-aside a:hover, .sr-aside a:focus-visible { border-bottom-color: var(--accent); }

        /* MOBILE IS A DESIGN STATE, NOT A STACKED DESKTOP (Build Protocol).
           Everything below is about making 390px scannable rather than
           merely narrow: single columns, tighter section rhythm, smaller
           card padding, and full-width controls. */
        @media (max-width: 760px) {
            main { padding-top: calc(var(--site-chrome) + 1.75rem); }
            .sr-routes { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 1rem; }
            .sr-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 0.9rem; }
            .sr-dots--two { columns: 1; }
            .sr-dots li { margin-bottom: 0.7rem; }
            .sr-section { margin-top: 2.1rem; }
            .sr-section > h2 { margin-bottom: 0.7rem; }
            .sr-section > .sr-intro { margin-bottom: 1.2rem; }
            .sr-cred { margin-top: 2rem; padding: 1.25rem 0; }
            .sr-card { padding: 1.4rem 1.25rem; }
            .sr-route { padding: 1.4rem 1.25rem; }
            .sr-step + .sr-step { margin-top: 1.25rem; padding-top: 1.25rem; }
            .sr-final { margin-top: 2.5rem; padding-top: 1.9rem; }
            .sr-final-actions .btn { width: 100%; text-align: center; }
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <%# currentPage carries a slug that is deliberately not in navPages: this
        is a campaign landing page and is not in the main navigation (Tom's
        decision of 25/09/2026), so nothing in the nav should highlight as
        active. The header and footer are otherwise the site's own, which is
        what the Build Protocol's assessment/tool rule requires. %>
    <%- include('partials/site-header', { currentPage: { slug: 'get-your-business-ready-to-sell' }, navPages, canEdit: false, content, pageContact }) %>

    <main>
        <span class="sr-label">Sale readiness</span>
        <h1>Get your business ready to sell</h1>
        <p class="sr-lede">Make the business less dependent on you, commercially stronger, and straightforward for somebody else to take over. That work pays whether you sell or keep it.</p>

        <%# OPERATOR CREDIBILITY, and every sentence here is controlled copy
            already published elsewhere on the site rather than written for
            this page. Tom's build instruction of 25/09/2026: "Use only
            controlled evidence about Tom having built, operated and sold a
            real business, with the approved wording available in the current
            controlled sources."

            Sentence 1 is the live row on the Business Consultant Devon page
            (casestudy.phase_3_body, third-person since the 01/08/2026 seed
            migration). Two differences from that row, both on Tom's own
            instruction: the opening connector "Separately," is dropped,
            because it referred to that page's preceding phase and means
            nothing here, and the subject is "Tom Arrington" where the row
            says "Tom", which is how Tom specified the sentence when he
            asked for this correction on 25/09/2026.

            Sentence 2 is the Brand Operating System's own PROOF wording.

            A third sentence, verbatim from views/market-ready-test-result.ejs
            ("Tom Arrington has bought, built and sold owner run businesses
            himself"), led this block until Tom removed it on 25/09/2026. It
            is recorded here rather than silently dropped, because it is
            approved copy and a future reader would otherwise have no way to
            know it was considered and cut on purpose.

            NOTHING here is a client case study and nothing existing has been
            reframed as sale-readiness work. These are facts about Tom's own
            business. %>
        <div class="sr-cred">
            <p>Tom Arrington built, grew and sold his own business in a seven-figure exit.</p>
            <p>More than 20 years of experience inside real businesses across Devon and Cornwall sits behind the work.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="sr-section">
            <h2>Which of these is you?</h2>
            <p class="sr-intro">The two need different first steps, so it is worth being honest about which one you are in.</p>

            <%# WHOLE-CARD CLICKABLE, with exactly ONE link per card.
                Tom's refinement pass of 25/09/2026: the whole card should go
                where its button goes, with the button still visible.

                Done with the stretched-link pattern rather than a wrapping
                anchor or a JS click handler, and the reason is accessibility.
                Wrapping the card in an <a> would either nest the button's
                anchor inside it (invalid HTML, undefined behaviour) or force
                the button to stop being a link. A JS handler on the card
                would give a mouse user a second way to navigate that a
                keyboard user does not have, and screen readers would announce
                one link while the card silently had another.

                Here the BUTTON IS THE ONLY LINK. Its ::after is stretched
                over the whole card, so a click anywhere lands on that same
                anchor. There is one tab stop per card, one announced link,
                one destination, and no duplicate navigation. The card shows
                the focus ring via :focus-within, so keyboard focus is visible
                at card level rather than only on the button.

                Known cost, accepted: the overlay sits above the card's text,
                so text inside a card is not selectable by dragging. That is
                the standard trade-off of this pattern and these cards carry
                two short sentences each. %>
            <div class="sr-routes">
                <div class="sr-route">
                    <span class="sr-route-label">A sale is years away</span>
                    <h3>You are preparing ahead</h3>
                    <p>Nothing is under way yet, which gives you time to act. Most of what makes a business straightforward to hand over takes a while to put right.</p>
                    <div class="sr-route-actions">
                        <a href="/where-to-start/commercial-review" class="btn btn-outline sr-route-link">The £500 Commercial Review</a>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <%# No sr-route--now modifier: see the equal-weight note in the
                    stylesheet. The class and its rule were both removed on
                    25/09/2026 rather than the rule being emptied, because a
                    class that styles nothing invites someone to "restore" it. %>
                <div class="sr-route">
                    <span class="sr-route-label">A sale is already under way</span>
                    <h3>You are already selling</h3>
                    <p>There is an offer on the table, or conversations have started. Speak to us before paying for a review, because some of what a buyer asks about cannot be put right inside a live process.</p>
                    <div class="sr-route-actions">
                        <a href="/book-a-30-minute-conversation" class="btn btn-outline sr-route-link">Book a 30 minute conversation</a>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="sr-section">
            <h2>What a buyer will want to understand</h2>
            <%# FIVE POINTS, AND ONLY FIVE. Rebuilt on 25/09/2026 from Tom's own
                commercial thinking, supplied point by point, replacing six
                items that had been written to fill a six-slot grid. The sixth
                ("The things you have not got to") was deleted because it was
                not Tom's thinking, and Tom was explicit that no sixth point may
                be manufactured to preserve the layout. The odd fifth item runs
                full width instead: see the .sr-item:last-child rule. %>
            <p class="sr-intro">A potential buyer will almost always come in with the same underlying questions, even if they phrase them differently. Owners often answer some of them well, but frequently fail to frame the business in the way a buyer needs to understand it.</p>
            <div class="sr-grid">
                <div class="sr-item">
                    <span class="sr-item-lead">Owner dependency</span>
                    <p>The real test is an unplanned absence. If you walked away for a week with no warning, what would you come back to? And would the answer be different if you had planned to be away? If you are effectively the product, there is very little for anyone else to buy. A business that can be sold has to be able to run without you.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="sr-item">
                    <span class="sr-item-lead">Whether the numbers tell the true story</span>
                    <p>Accounts can make a business look successful while hiding what the owner's own contribution really costs. An owner working 100 hours a week for free can prop up profitability that is not really there. A serious buyer sees through that very early.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="sr-item">
                    <span class="sr-item-lead">Revenue distribution and deal risk</span>
                    <p>How the revenue is distributed can matter as much as how much depends on you. Too much resting on one customer, one supplier or one relationship can become a serious obstacle to a sale. Finding that early gives you time to do something about it, rather than meeting it in the middle of a transaction.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="sr-item">
                    <span class="sr-item-lead">What is actually adding value</span>
                    <p>Bigger does not necessarily mean more valuable. Bloated or loss-making parts of a business can take up people, time and capacity while hurting its value. A fresh and commercially savage set of eyes should tell you what strengthens the business and what may be better cut before a sale.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="sr-item">
                    <span class="sr-item-lead">Transferability</span>
                    <p>This is not simply about having procedures written down. To hand a business over properly, the recurring actions, judgements and decisions that currently live in your head have to be explainable, and then transferable to somebody else.</p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <%# THE COMMERCIAL REVIEW IS EXPLAINED ONCE, ON ITS OWN PAGE. Until
            25/09/2026 this section restated the product (the conversation,
            the written assessment, four business days, the £500 credit) as a
            second description of the same thing. Tom's decision: say only why
            the existing Review is relevant here, and link to the canonical
            page for the mechanics, so there are never two descriptions of
            one product to drift apart. %>
        <div class="sr-section">
            <h2>Where the Commercial Review comes in</h2>
            <p>The £500 Commercial Review is where we look at the business through the eyes of a potential buyer and identify what is genuinely worth dealing with before the buyer gets the chance to pick it apart.</p>
            <p class="sr-aside"><a href="/where-to-start/commercial-review">How the Commercial Review works <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></a></p>
        </div>

        <%# No shopping list, deliberately. Tom's decision of 25/09/2026: the
            problems are already named in the buyer section, and listing them
            again as work we could do reads as an upsell. The AI and technology
            paragraph went too, as unnecessary on this page. %>
        <div class="sr-section">
            <h2>What happens after the Review</h2>
            <p>The Review tells us where the problems are. Then we decide what is actually worth fixing before a buyer arrives.</p>
            <p>Everything after that is judged on one question: will dealing with it strengthen what the business offers a buyer, protect its value, improve its value, or materially help the sale complete?</p>
            <p>We spend your time and money where there is a commercial reason to. We do not fix things simply because they could be improved.</p>
            <p>Any further work is agreed separately, once the Review has established what is worth doing and where the value is.</p>
        </div>

        <%# WE STAY IN YOUR CORNER. Copy rebuilt on 25/09/2026 from Tom's own
            proposition, replacing generic wording.

            NO PANEL, deliberately. Until 25/09/2026 this copy sat in the
            page's one cream "paper" card. Tom inspected it live and removed
            it: against the navy page the large cream box was too heavy and
            made the section read as a generic information card. The copy
            now sits directly on the page background in the ordinary body
            text, held to a readable measure, with a short gold rule under
            the heading and spacing (not a container) doing the separating.
            Do not put it back in a card or a paper surface: see .sr-corner.

            The professional boundary now sits in the fifth paragraph: where
            the job genuinely needs an accountant, solicitor, valuer or other
            specialist, the owner should use one. The first paragraph says we
            fight the owner's corner "for as long as we are involved", which
            Tom approved to tie that to our role rather than to the
            transaction, without adding a disclaimer. The page still does not
            say in as many words that Arrington is not a broker or an exit
            planner; that was Tom's call and it is his to reverse.

            The fire line was translated, not dropped: the Brand Operating
            System bans fire-based business metaphors, so "the building is on
            fire" became "the foundations are giving way", keeping Tom's
            principle. The closing paragraph is the outcome Tom cares about,
            with the price disclaimer attached so it cannot read as a promise
            about what a buyer will pay. %>
        <div class="sr-section sr-corner">
            <h2>We stay in your corner</h2>
            <div class="sr-corner-body">
                <p>Selling a business can be brutal, both emotionally and commercially. We stay alongside you and fight for your commercial interests for as long as we are involved.</p>
                <p>Tom has been through the process himself, and that experience is part of the value of having us in your corner.</p>
                <p>Before a buyer gets the chance to pick the business apart, we want to do it first, finding the weaknesses that could cost you money and strengthening what the buyer is actually going to be paying for.</p>
                <p>In a sale, the business itself is the product. We want to make that product as strong as is commercially sensible, but every intervention has to earn its place. There is no point fixing the roof if the foundations are giving way.</p>
                <p>We protect every point and every penny. Where the job genuinely needs an accountant, a solicitor, a valuer or another specialist, use one. But nobody should spend £5,000 on something that can competently be done for £500, simply because they are in the middle of a sale. We question the costs and the decisions, and we protect your money as though it were our own.</p>
                <p>What we care about is that you come out of it knowing somebody fought your corner and protected every penny of value the business deserved. What a buyer finally pays is theirs to decide, not ours.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <%# OWNER DEPENDENCY, placed immediately before the closing CTA on
            Tom's instruction of 25/09/2026. Since the same day's copy rebuild
            it is a BRIDGE, not an argument: the explanation lives in the
            "Owner dependency" buyer point above, and saying it twice read as
            an upsell. It keeps Tom's two lines and hands the visitor a free,
            concrete next step that is not a purchase.

            The destination is the EXISTING route, inspected before writing:
            /owner-dependency-quiz, registered in server.js and listed in
            sitemap.xml. Nothing new was invented. "Owner Dependency Quiz" is
            its exact public name and the Brand Operating System forbids
            renaming it, so the supporting line names it precisely while the
            button carries Tom's own wording.

            Outline rather than filled, deliberately: the conversation below
            is still the page's primary call to action and this must not
            outrank it. %>
        <div class="sr-section">
            <h2>If the business depends on you, start there</h2>
            <%# ILLUSTRATIVE IMAGE, AND THE PROVENANCE MATTERS MORE THAN THE
                PLACEMENT. Tom's instruction of 25/09/2026, recorded verbatim
                because a later reader could easily mistake this for a client
                photograph: "the image is AI-generated specifically for
                Arrington Consultancy for this page. It is illustrative and
                must not be presented or positioned as evidence of a real
                client, real business or Arrington engagement."

                So it sits beside an ARGUMENT, never beside a claim. This
                section makes a case about owner dependency and names no
                client, no outcome and no figure. It is deliberately NOT in
                the hero, not near the credibility block, and not near the
                Evidence link, because in any of those places a reader would
                reasonably take it as documentary.

                The alt text says "illustration" in its first word for the
                same reason. The site already has this convention: the Abacus
                photo in index.ejs is described as "representative of" its
                case study rather than as a record of it.

                One image only, on Tom's instruction. Do not add the second
                generated image he sent, or any other, to this page.

                Sizes: the column caps at 700px, so 1400 is the 2x case and
                700 the 1x; nothing larger is ever served. The 1.78MB PNG
                source is not shipped. width/height are set so the space is
                reserved before it loads and the copy underneath does not
                jump. %>
            <picture>
                <source
                    type="image/webp"
                    srcset="/img/sale-readiness/owner-watching-700.webp 700w, /img/sale-readiness/owner-watching-1400.webp 1400w"
                    sizes="(max-width: 760px) calc(100vw - 3rem), 700px">
                <img
                    class="sr-figure"
                    src="/img/sale-readiness/owner-watching-1400.jpg"
                    width="1400" height="700"
                    loading="lazy" decoding="async"
                    alt="Illustration of a business owner standing back in a workshop doorway, watching staff load vans and work in the yard at the end of the day">
            </picture>
            <div class="sr-card">
                <p>If the business owns you, it is going to be very difficult to sell it. And the business cannot sell you with it. It is worth knowing how much of it still comes back to you.</p>
                <div class="sr-route-actions">
                    <a href="/owner-dependency-quiz" class="btn btn-outline">See how dependent the business is on you</a>
                </div>
                <p class="sr-aside">The Owner Dependency Quiz, about five minutes, no charge.</p>
